The callers. A gate on a definition is half a seam.

Six milestones of this port have gated a Linux-kernel implementation out of
the build and left every place that NAMES it behind. A -k 0 build cannot see
that, because a compile succeeds against a declaration - it only appears when
something links, and //content is the first thing here big enough to.

  M148  gated mojo's ChannelLinux on has_linux_kernel
        mojo/core/embedder/embedder.cc still called
        ChannelLinux::SetSharedMemParameters

  M159  gated gl_fence_android_native_fence_sync.cc on has_linux_kernel,
        because it is libsync
        ui/gl/gl_fence.cc still #defined
        USE_GL_FENCE_ANDROID_NATIVE_FENCE_SYNC from the platform family, and
        that #define is what decides whether the three GLFence functions call
        into the file that is no longer there

  M161  gated os_metrics_linux.cc - /proc/<pid>/smaps and
        prctl(PR_SET_DUMPABLE)
        two callers of OSMetrics::FillOSMemoryDump remained, and every
        implementation of it is per-KERNEL rather than per-family, so a
        platform in a family whose kernel is its own matched none of them.
        os_metrics.cc gets the arm for that case: false, which is what
        os_metrics_fuchsia.cc already returns when it has nothing to report,
        and which makes client_process_impl.cc log the failure it is written
        to log. Filling the struct with zeroes instead would put a process
        using no memory into about:memory.

  M161  gated media/capture's video/linux, which is V4L2
        create_video_capture_device_factory.cc still named
        VideoCaptureDeviceFactoryLinux. Chromium's own #else for a platform
        with no capture backend is NOTIMPLEMENTED() and a null factory, which
        is the truth about a machine with no camera - and getUserMedia then
        fails with the error the specification defines, which is M161's own
        argument for why this is not a pretence.

  M163  gated the five seccomp pre-sandbox hooks and SandboxLinux
        content/gpu/gpu_main.cc still called
        SandboxLinux::InitializeSandbox with GpuPreSandboxHook, and
        content/utility/services.cc still called
        sandbox::InitLibcLocaltimeFunctions - which exists to resolve
        localtime before a seccomp policy closes /etc/localtime.

None of these is a new decision. Each is the question an earlier milestone
already answered, asked in the one more place that names the answer.

